{
  "name": "intent",
  "_comment": "Epic O - free-text utterance -> structured wingman command. The grammar below is the SHIPPED vocabulary: it mirrors fl::ai::WingmanCommand (engine/ai/WingmanCommand.h), which is the single source of truth, and tests/test_ai_eval.py asserts the two match - so a rename in the engine fails here. The out-of-grammar cases (expect 'unknown') are the load-bearing ones: an agent that maps an unsupported request onto a real command is worse than one that declines, because execution goes through the grammar with no human in the loop. 'unknown' is a DECLINE sentinel and is deliberately NOT an executable command - fl::ai::parseWingmanCommand rejects it.",
  "budget_s": 2.0,
  "grammar": [
    "attack_my_target",
    "engage_bandits",
    "rejoin",
    "cover_me",
    "hold_fire",
    "return_to_base",
    "unknown"
  ],
  "system_prompt": "Map a pilot's radio call to one wingman command.\n\nattack_my_target: attack the target the lead designated\nengage_bandits: engage hostile aircraft at will\nrejoin: return to formation on the lead\ncover_me: defensive support for the lead\nhold_fire: weapons hold, do not fire\nreturn_to_base: disengage and fly home\nunknown: the call matches none of the above\n\nReply with only {\"command\": \"<one of the above>\"}. Never invent a command. If the call is ambiguous or outside the list, answer \"unknown\" - declining is correct and preferred over guessing.",
